---
source: tests/snapshots_help_contracts.rs
expression: "help(&scratch, None, &[\"--help\", \"worktree\", \"prune\"])"
---
Usage: worktree prune [--repo PATH] [--stale-days N] [--release-stale --reason TEXT] [--dryrun] [--json|--format json] [--remove] [--no-sync]

Global, removal-by-default cleanup of managed worktrees, grouped by canonical directory. Without --repo it enumerates every repository identity the local lease store records (even outside Git) and resolves each through its main checkout, skipping an identity it cannot verify with a bounded reason instead of guessing; --repo PATH restricts the pass to one verified checkout. A directory is removed as soon as every issue associated with it is confirmed closed on the configured provider, no active lease references the canonical directory, and prune's running-session guard clears it: only a session the connected shared foreground-drain service reports as *currently running* in the directory blocks removal, checked read-only, so an inactive historical session record that still points at the directory never keeps it. Prune never enumerates historical sessions and never moves a session; coverage is that one connected service, not a whole-machine multi-service inventory. An unavailable, malformed, mismatched, or non-absolute running-session answer, or any running session (root or child) at the directory, keeps it; removal then uses `git worktree remove` without `--force` after a fresh re-check of the running-session answer and fresh lease rows. There is no waiting period: a freshly retained closed clean directory is removed immediately, so --stale-days never gates directory deletion and exists only for the explicit --release-stale recovery below. Closure is read from the configured provider through the same current-configured-provider resolution the rest of the CLI uses; a missing, unavailable, ambiguous, or mismatched issue is never treated as closed, and a preview may read closure but never mutates. The local guards — never the main checkout, never a path an `active` lease points at, never dirty or untracked — are decided before any running-session query, so a main-checkout or dirty directory makes zero host or provider calls. Because the runtime-host dependency is explicit, an unreadable service keeps even a pure-CLI candidate; the human failure count scopes to failed `git worktree remove` calls rather than to kept directories, which can include an unproven running-session answer. --dryrun (alias --dry-run) is the read-only preview: it performs the same read-only running-session discovery to report eligibility, but never mutates any directory, lease row, or session and never moves a session. --release-stale requires a non-empty --reason unless it is a preview and flips exactly the active leases whose heartbeat is older than --stale-days to `retained` in a single transaction; a directory that held an active lease at invocation start is never removed by the same call, and --reason without --release-stale is rejected. The default stdout is concise English text with directory-based counts; --json or --format json selects the machine-readable aggregate envelope (per repository plus totals). --remove is an accepted compatibility no-op and --no-sync is accepted but no longer runs a sync pass: prune never syncs. Branches and lease rows are never deleted. A few `worktree prune:` progress lines stream to stderr, flushed before the potentially slow host and closure checks so a long scan is visibly working, while stdout stays reserved for the final text or the single JSON document; within one invocation the read-only scan shares a repeated host answer, but every removal re-checks the host live. Orchestrator-only.
